# Line endings, settled in the repo rather than per machine.
#
# Git for Windows ships `core.autocrlf=true` (set system-wide on this machine), so EVERY checkout,
# `git stash pop`, merge or `git checkout -- <file>` rewrites the working tree to CRLF — while
# prettier's `endOfLine` default is `lf`, and both `pnpm lint` and the pre-commit hook demand LF.
# That is the whole "prettier suddenly fails on files nobody edited" loop, and the reason `git add`
# printed "LF will be replaced by CRLF" on every commit. A file in the repo fixes it for every
# clone and for CI; a local git config fixes it for one machine and forgets.
#
# The repo has ALWAYS stored LF (368 of 368 text files), so this normalises nothing — it only stops
# the working tree from being handed something else.
* text=auto eol=lf

# CSVs are exempt, because for content files the bytes ARE the format: BOM + CRLF for Excel and
# Cyrillic safety, and a `#content-hash` computed over them. The packs we ship live in the content
# repo (which pins `* -text` so blob-SHA diffing works at all); the only CSV tracked here is a test
# fixture standing in for a user's file, and a fixture that quietly loses its CRLF is a test that
# proves the opposite of what it claims.
*.csv -text
